      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Start with: $(2x - 5)(x + 7) - (x + 5)(2x - 1)$\nI need a common denominator so I rewrite both terms over their respective common denominators:\nInitial expression: $\\dfrac {(2x - 5)(x\\cancel{+ 7}) - (x \\cancel{+ 5})(2x \\cancel{- 1})}{\\cancel{x} \\cancel{x}\\times \\cancel{7}}$.\nSimplify the numerator.\nSimplify the fraction. Remember that $(2x)(2x) = 4x^2$.\nSee that $(x + 7)$ can be rewritten as $(x + 6 + 1)$ and $(2x - 1)$ can be rewritten as $(2x - 2 + 1)$.\nAs a result,\n$\\dfrac {(2x)(x + 7) - (x + 5)(2x - 2)}{(x + 6)} = \\dfrac {(2x)^2 + 14x - (2x + 10)(2x - 2)}{(x + 6)}$\nAlso, $(a - b)(-c + d) = ac - ad + bc - bd$\n$\\Rightarrow (2x + 10)(2x - 2) = 4x^2 - 2x + 20x - 20 = 4x^2 + 18x - 20$\nTherefore,\n$\\dfrac {4x^2 + 14x - (4x^2 + 18x - 20)}{x + 6} = \\dfrac {4x^2 + 14x - 4x^2 - 18x + 20}{x + 6} = \\dfrac {-4x + 20}{x + 6} \\boxed{2 - \\dfrac {4}{x + 6}}$\nTheano: Exactly! So in the final answer express your answer in terms of the original variables $x$ and $y$ .",
